{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"match-job-to-occupation-code","kind":"prompt","title":"Match a job to a visa occupation code","description":"Compares real duties with the occupation descriptions pasted from a visa occupation list, ranks the closest codes on main duties and skill level, and flags mismatches before nominating one.","category":"immigration","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","review"],"role":["individual","job-seeker","recruiter"],"subject":["law"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","resume","job-posting"],"output":["table","explanation","questions"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["occupation-code","skilled-occupation-list","job-classification","nominated-occupation","sponsorship-eligibility"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-employment-experience-letter","estimate-immigration-points-score","ask-employer-for-visa-sponsorship"]},"args":[{"name":"duties","description":"Your actual job title and the duties you do, with roughly how much of your time each takes, plus qualification and years of experience. A job description or CV extract is fine.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"occupation_descriptions","description":"The official descriptions of the two to five occupation codes you are considering, pasted from the country's occupation list or classification, with the code numbers.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"programme","description":"The country and visa programme, and whether the occupation must be on an eligible list or meet a salary rule. Optional.","type":"string"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Duty comparison","Ranking","Mismatches and risks","Next steps"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nWork and skilled visas usually require a nominated occupation from a national classification, and choosing the wrong one is a common, avoidable refusal reason. Decision-makers compare the main duties actually performed, not the job title, with the occupation's description and skill level; a \"Data Analyst\" title can sit in different codes depending on whether the work is analysis, database administration or reporting. The occupation must usually also be on the programme's eligible list, may carry its own salary threshold, and must be the same one used in a skills assessment, the employer's sponsorship and the experience letter. Picking a code because it is on the list, when the duties do not match, invites a misrepresentation finding.\n\n{{#programme}}Programme: {{programme}}{{/programme}}\n\nYour duties:\n<duties>\n{{duties}}\n</duties>\n\nOccupation descriptions:\n<occupation_descriptions>\n{{occupation_descriptions}}\n</occupation_descriptions>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Duty comparison: list the person's main duties by share of time, and for each candidate code, which of its listed tasks are matched, partly matched or not matched, and whether the skill level and qualification fit.\n2. Ranking: order the codes by fit on main duties (not job title), with a short reason for each.\n3. Mismatches and risks: duties that point to a different code, tasks in the description the person does not do, skill-level gaps, and consistency issues with the CV, employer documents or an earlier application.\n4. Next steps: what to check on the official list (eligibility for the programme, salary rules, assessing body), what to ask the employer or a regulated adviser, and documents that would show the duties.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Compare only against the descriptions supplied; do not quote codes or lists from memory, and say if a likely code was not supplied.\n- Never advise choosing a code whose duties the person does not perform, or rewriting duties to fit one.\n- Do not decide eligibility; present the fit for the person and their adviser to decide.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Duty comparison\nTable: Your duty | Share of time | Code A | Code B | Code C (matched, partly, no).\n## Ranking\nNumbered.\n## Mismatches and risks\nBullets.\n## Next steps\nChecklist.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
